  - model: {fileID: 2242888416370347441, guid: 5ea2cab9103b26d928caeabb8c0eb8c8, type: 3}
    idle: {fileID: -6736537688726264772, guid: 5ea2cab9103b26d928caeabb8c0eb8c8, type: 3}
    talk: {fileID: -1208920132165896793, guid: 5ea2cab9103b26d928caeabb8c0eb8c8, type: 3}
    wave: {fileID: -469540091750951958, guid: 5ea2cab9103b26d928caeabb8c0eb8c8, type: 3}
    height: 1.76
  - model: {fileID: 2242888416370347441, guid: c81ce8f0810e04ddab30b22ea28f1750, type: 3}
    idle: {fileID: -6736537688726264772, guid: c81ce8f0810e04ddab30b22ea28f1750, type: 3}
    talk: {fileID: -1208920132165896793, guid: c81ce8f0810e04ddab30b22ea28f1750, type: 3}
    wave: {fileID: -469540091750951958, guid: c81ce8f0810e04ddab30b22ea28f1750, type: 3}
    height: 1.65
  teamSize: 10
  directorDistance: 3
  directorSide: 0.25
  missions:
  - title: Sky Sensor
    subtitle: EASY  |  A tiny probe that measures the sky
    briefingLines:
    - 'Your mission: <b>Sky Sensor</b>.'
    - 'You will build a CanSat: a tiny satellite the size of a soda can.'
    - A small rocket carries it about 1 kilometre up. Then it pops out and floats down on a parachute.
    - 'On the way down, it measures the air: how warm it is, and how hard it presses.'
    - 'Fun fact: the air gets about 6.5 degrees colder for every kilometre you go up!'
    - Choose the parachute carefully. Too small and it crashes. Too big and it drifts away.
    summaryEmail: '<b><color=#8AB4F8>YOUR MISSION</color></b>

      Sky Sensor

      A tiny probe that measures the sky


      <b><color=#8AB4F8>THE PLAN</color></b>

      - Fly about 1 km up

      - Pop out and open the parachute

      - Measure the air on the way down

      - Land in one piece


      <b><color=#8AB4F8>WHAT TO BUILD</color></b>

      Rocket: a small one

      CanSat: sensors, battery, radio, parachute'
  - title: Earth Watcher
    subtitle: MEDIUM  |  A satellite that takes photos of Earth
    briefingLines:
    - 'Your mission: <b>Earth Watcher</b>.'
    - You will put a satellite into orbit, about 700 kilometres above the Earth.
    - To stay up there it must fly about 27,000 km per hour. That is one lap around Earth every 100 minutes!
    - Its camera will watch our farms, forests and coasts, and help us after typhoons.
    - It needs solar panels for power, a radio to send photos home, and a way to point its camera.
    - 'Remember: every extra kilogram needs a bigger rocket. Keep it light!'
    summaryEmail: '<b><color=#8AB4F8>YOUR MISSION</color></b>

      Earth Watcher

      A satellite that takes photos of Earth


      <b><color=#8AB4F8>THE PLAN</color></b>

      - Launch into orbit, 700 km up

      - Open the solar panels

      - Take photos of Earth

      - Send them home


      <b><color=#8AB4F8>WHAT TO BUILD</color></b>

      Rocket: small or medium

      Satellite: camera, solar panels, battery, radio'
  - title: Moon Mapper
    subtitle: HARD  |  A spacecraft that maps the Moon
    briefingLines:
    - 'Your mission: <b>Moon Mapper</b>. Our hardest one yet!'
    - Your spacecraft will travel 384,000 kilometres to the Moon. The trip takes about 3 to 5 days.
    - When it arrives, it must fire its engine to slow down. Too fast, and it flies right past the Moon!
    - Then it circles just 50 kilometres above the surface and maps every crater and mountain.
    - Fuel is everything here. You need a big rocket, but you have a small budget.
    summaryEmail: '<b><color=#8AB4F8>YOUR MISSION</color></b>

      Moon Mapper

      A spacecraft that maps the Moon


      <b><color=#8AB4F8>THE PLAN</color></b>

      - Launch into orbit around Earth

      - Fly to the Moon (3-5 days)

      - Slow down and circle the Moon

      - Map the surface and send the maps home


      <b><color=#8AB4F8>WHAT TO BUILD</color></b>

      Rocket: a powerful one

      Spacecraft: camera, laser, engine, fuel, solar panels, radio'
  onBriefingFinished:
    m_PersistentCalls:
